+# Decision: Self-Hosted WebSockets for Change Delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard delivers committed board changes from editors to up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers across 40 weekend markets. The delivery mechanism must meet a 15-second freshness target under normal conditions, handle opening-time bursts of 20 edits/second, and recover browsers disconnected for up to 20 minutes. Three delivery approaches exist in the domain; the choice shapes the change store, delivery component, and browser sync component.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gates
+
+- **No vendor lock-in** — the approach must not bind the board to a specific realtime provider's API or pricing model. (Eliminates hosted realtime service.)
+- **Subsecond latency achievable** — the approach must be capable of delivering updates well within the 15-second target, ideally subsecond. (All three options pass.)
+
+### Weighted Criteria
+
+| Criterion | Importance | Tenet |
+|---|---|---|
+| Recurring operational cost | Major | Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted |
+| Connection operations burden | Major | — |
+| Latency headroom | Moderate | — |
+| Implementation complexity | Moderate | — |
+| Reconnection / catch-up robustness | Major | Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board |
+| Scale to 800 concurrent browsers | Major | Support 800 simultaneous browsers |
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Option 1: Five-Second HTTPS Polling
+
+**Description** — Browsers poll the server every 5 seconds via HTTPS for new revisions since their last known cursor. The change store provides cursor-based queries. Delivery is stateless request/response. Browsers run a simple polling loop.
+
+**Analysis**
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — no persistent connections, minimal server resources.
+- Connection operations burden: **strong** — zero connection management, no reconnect logic.
+- Latency headroom: **weak** — 5-second fixed interval; worst-case 5s + processing latency.
+- Implementation complexity: **strong** — simplest of the three; stateless, well-understood.
+- Reconnection robustness: **strong** — no persistent connection to lose; poll loop naturally retries.
+- Scale to 800 browsers: **partial** — 800 browsers × 12 polls/min = ~160 req/s sustained; bursts multiply.
+
+**Summary** — Simplest operations, but inherent 5-second latency floor and polling load at scale.
+
+### Option 2: Self-Hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description** — Persistent WebSocket connections from each browser to the server. The change store provides replayable revisions and a publication outbox. Delivery manages connection lifecycle (accept, heartbeat, backpressure, close), reconnection with revision-based catch-up, and fan-out of committed edits. Browser sync holds a persistent subscription, auto-reconnects with last-known revision, and replays missed edits.
+
+**Analysis**
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — no vendor fees; infrastructure cost only.
+- Connection operations burden: **weak** — we own 800 persistent connections, heartbeats, backpressure, graceful drain, and reconnect storms.
+- Latency headroom: **strong** — true push; subsecond delivery typical.
+- Implementation complexity: **weak** — connection management, replay, and reconnect logic are non-trivial.
+- Reconnection robustness: **strong** — revision-based catch-up handles arbitrary disconnect durations (up to 20 min goal).
+- Scale to 800 browsers: **strong** — persistent connections handle burst fan-out efficiently; no poll amplification.
+
+**Summary** — Best latency and cost profile; accepts significant connection-management and reconnect engineering.
+
+### Option 3: Hosted Realtime Service (e.g., Pusher, Ably, Firebase)
+
+**Description** — Server publishes committed edits to a managed realtime provider; browsers subscribe via the provider's SDK. The change store reconciles publications for audit. Delivery wraps the provider's publish API. Browser sync uses the provider's subscription and presence model.
+
+**Analysis**
+- Recurring cost: **eliminated** — gate failure: recurring vendor fees and provider coupling.
+- Connection operations burden: **strong** — provider owns connections, scaling, and reconnect.
+- Latency headroom: **strong** — provider networks optimized for low-latency fan-out.
+- Implementation complexity: **strong** — thin wrapper around provider SDK; minimal custom logic.
+- Reconnection robustness: **strong** — provider handles reconnect and catch-up.
+- Scale to 800 browsers: **strong** — provider infrastructure handles scale.
+
+**Summary** — Fastest to operationalize, but fails the no-vendor-lock-in gate and incurs recurring fees.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: Self-Hosted WebSockets (Option 2).**
+
+This option wins on the two major criteria that are gates for this project: **no vendor lock-in** and **recurring operational cost**. It also provides the strongest latency headroom and scale characteristics, which matter for the opening-time burst requirement (20 edits/second across 800 browsers).
+
+The tradeoff is accepted: we take on **connection operations burden** (major) and **implementation complexity** (moderate) — specifically, WebSocket session management, heartbeat/backpressure, reconnection logic, and revision-based catch-up replay. These become first-class responsibilities of the delivery component and browser sync component, and they require the change store to provide a replayable revision log and reliable publication outbox.
+
+Tenets engaged:
+- "Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted" — we chose self-hosted to avoid recurring cost and lock-in.
+- "Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board" — revision-based catch-up makes disconnect duration explicit; the browser can show "reconnecting…" with last-known revision age.
+
+Downstream obligations:
+- **Change store**: must provide replayable revisions (by revision number) and a reliable publication outbox for delivery to consume.
+- **Delivery**: owns WebSocket session lifecycle, fan-out, backpressure, and catch-up replay on reconnect.
+- **Browser sync**: implements persistent subscription, automatic reconnect with last-known revision, and in-order replay of missed edits.
